        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\n\nI could indeed reproduce this with exactly 2% CPU load (which was surprising to see the *exact* match). The reason for this is that 'mini.pick' has a [custom key query process](https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-pick.html#minipick-overview-implementationdetails) which is different from regular Insert or Command-line modes. It uses [`getcharstr()`](https://neovim.io/doc/user/helptag.html?tag=getcharstr()) function to get user's keys. It has considerable benefits, but the cost is that in fixes the screen state and it doesn't redraw automatically. To counter this, there is an constant asynchronous explicit `:redraw`, by default every 10 ms.\n\nThe solution that worked for me is to increase [`config.delay.async`](https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-pick.html#minipick.config-delays) to make Neovim redraw less frequently. This will have no effect on using picker per se, but if there are changes outside of it (like notifications or progress reports) they will be shown only on the next explicit redraw. The default 10 ms is chosen for a smooth default experience since 1-2% CPU load for something that is called on demand and is not very long lived seems acceptable.\n\nClosing as I am pretty sure that this is working as expected, but could you please confirm that increasing `config.delay.async` also reduces the CPU load for you?",
